# 06_Journal
## Phase 2: The Architect 
### Chapter 6: The Prospectus

The idea that stood out to me the most in this chapter was that narrowing a research question takes more than making it specific. The funnel example starts with the broad idea of studying Twitch chat and ends with a question about directed messages in gaming and non-gaming streams. At first, narrowing that much seemed like giving up the more interesting parts of the topic. The chapter helped me see why it makes a stronger study. The final version says what evidence to collect, what comparison to make, and when the project is finished. 

I also had to think more carefully about operationalization. The parasocial relationship example shows why this is difficult. Donation or hours watched are easy to count, but they do not necessarily prove that a viewer feels an emotional bond with a streamer. Chat messages may show expressions of familiarity, but they leave out viewers who never post. Every measure captures part of the concept and misses something else. That made me wonder: how do researchers decide when a measure is good enough to support their claim? It seems important to explain what the evidence can show without treating it as a complete picture. 

The connection between the prospectus and pre-registration changed how I viewed the assignment. I initially thought of the prospectus as a short description of a project that could be changed freely later. Now I see that recording the question and method before analyzing results can help prevent a researcher from changing the plan just to make a finding look stronger. For me, the chapter's main lesson is that a focused plan makes a study both more manageable and more honest.
